WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:05.740
Rodger: My name's Rodger Cornell.

00:00:05.948 --> 00:00:07.056
I'm an explosives engineer.

00:00:07.056 --> 00:00:09.280
Declan: Boom shakalaka!

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.193
Rodger: All right, so right off the bat

00:00:24.193 --> 00:00:25.293
there's a lot of gunfire.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.823
You see holes being ripped
into multiple fuel tanks.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:34.760
Essentially just the
perfect environment to start

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.740
some kind of combustion process.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.300
So you can see that there's
at least some source of flame

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.950
under the hood of the engine bay.

00:00:50.366 --> 00:00:54.570
So, oh man, that explosion
is just ridiculous.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.340
We just saw bullets ripping through

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.670
almost every fuel tank there.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.740
Those tanks, they no
longer have any capacity

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.710
for holding pressure, which
will prevent the reaction

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.380
from accelerating and turning

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.120
into something violent like this.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.953
And I know it's The Rock, but no one is

00:01:10.953 --> 00:01:12.943
just going to walk away from that thing.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.520
The villain in this movie
is essentially trying

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.770
to create a distraction,

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.780
so he's thinking that by
lighting the fuel source

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.120
of an automobile on
fire that he can somehow

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.040
create this explosion and that's going

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.550
to distract everyone inside.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.270
He takes a rag and inserts
it into the gas tank

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.600
to get it soaked with gasoline,

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.090
and then you'll actually see him take

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:42.470
this combustible material
and use it to cover up

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.293
the actual gas cap so that
no gas vapors can be exposed.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:50.580
The big issue with this
scene is the fact that

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.770
I can't see why the
gasoline would actually

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.660
go through an explosive vent.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.020
Even if you have a flame that exists

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.160
at that gas cap interface,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:01.390
there's no reason for it
to travel into the tank.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:04.240
I would expect the flame
to just sit at that gas cap

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.600
and just continue to burn until there were

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.720
no more gasoline vapors to pull from.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.490
You would essentially
have a giant car candle.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.520
In order to actually get it to explode,

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.480
if I had to do it, I would
try to seal up that gas tank

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.560
as best as I can, and if
you put a flame underneath

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.360
that gas tank, and if
you start to heat it up,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.030
increase pressure to the
point that it ruptures,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.090
and then all of that
would light up and create

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.423
somewhat of an explosion.

00:02:37.563 --> 00:02:38.396
James Bond: Let's go home.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.490
Rodger: So "James Bond"
movies are typically filled

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.370
with ridiculous scenes that just

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:48.893
are completely nonphysical.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.463
Bond: I said, doesn't time fly?

00:02:57.905 --> 00:03:00.880
Rodger: The watch that was just
thrown is one of the gadgets

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.803
that James Bond got from Q, I believe?

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.413
Q: But you can have this.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:10.980
Bond: Does it do anything?

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.540
Rodger: Supposedly it's
filled with some sort

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.030
of energetic material, and it
can be used as a detonator.

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:17.470
Q: It tells the time.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:20.480
Rodger: There's no device
that can be that small

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.810
and yet that potent to
actually knock someone

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.490
out of their chair, throw
them across the room,

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:27.330
there's certainly a lot
of fantasy, but at least

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.730
there's some cool physical elements within

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.710
the actual flame production that we see.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.330
The one advantage that this scene has

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.960
is I'm pretty sure there's
no CGI or limited CGI here.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.460
You can see the black smoke.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:41.360
The black that you're actually
seeing is soot production,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.280
essentially carbon in the air.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.150
If you look into the fireball,

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.880
you can see these liquid jets of fuel

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:50.830
being propelled into the air.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.590
When I saw like the fuel
shooting out, I was like, "Ah,

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.890
this is definitely real,
this took a lot of work."

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.160
Q: Magnificent isn't she?

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.730
Rodger: The explosion is
almost perfectly symmetric.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:02.350
Starts at the center, breaks
down the legs at the same rate.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:05.210
Combustion events like this,
especially if it's an accident,

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:08.130
they're unpredictable,
there's a million factors,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.870
there's a lot of physical
considerations to take into place,

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:14.070
you can bet that there were
charges that were timed

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.300
in a very symmetric
fashion so that the flame

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.393
looked exactly as it does here.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.010
This poor, poor man has been

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.390
given essentially a suppository.

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.830
John Creasy: Used by convicts
to hide money and drugs.

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.190
Rodger: Filled with C4, which is a really

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.420
commonly used energetic material.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.560
It's been inserted into him.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:45.990
This is going to get a little dark.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:46.823
Creasy: What do you got?

00:04:46.823 --> 00:04:47.900
Rodger: The human body is actually

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.900
a really great tamping mechanism.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.150
We're made of water, and
we have pockets of air,

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.160
and both of which do a really good job

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.280
of dampening out shock waves.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.520
The guy would just have a really bad day.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.310
And don't get me wrong,
he'd certainly be dead,

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.560
but for the most part there's no reason

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.360
a fuel tank would explode.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.160
If you had that amount of material,

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:11.200
it just wouldn't do that kind of damage.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.561
Way too much flame, the
explosion's way too big.

00:05:25.635 --> 00:05:27.910
He shoots a bullet into the concrete

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:30.563
to get this trail of fuel to start.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:34.960
Bullets don't spark unless you have

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.530
very, very specific rounds.
This wouldn't work at all.

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:40.350
If I had to guess what
would happen if you actually

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:42.190
were able to light that trail of fuel,

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.400
I'd imagine the car would
just sit there and burn.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.120
It'd get hot, it would burn for a while,

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.710
it might melt some of the
surrounding structure,

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:49.543
and that's it.

00:05:49.543 --> 00:05:51.140
The fact again that
there's fuel on the ground

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.200
tells me that the fuel tank is ruptured.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.290
There's no real mechanism
for building pressure

00:05:56.290 --> 00:05:57.723
in that combustion system.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:01.593
Bond: Let's go home.

